What is the historical context of Ælfgar, Earl of Mercia, including his family relationships and position of power?
Ælfgar, Earl of Mercia, was a 11th-century English nobleman who lived during the reign of King Edward the Confessor. He was the son of Leofric, Earl of Mercia, and his mother was Godgifu (Lady Godiva), a famous and influential woman of her time. Ælfgar succeeded his father as Earl of Mercia in 1057 and later gained the additional title of Earl of East Anglia.

He was exiled for a time, but during his exile, he profited from the downfall of Earl Godwin of Wessex. Ælfgar's daughter married Gruffydd ap Llywelyn, the Welsh king, and later became the wife of King Harold, who was defeated at the Battle of Hastings in 1066.

Ælfgar's family relationships and position of power are significant in understanding the complex web of alliances and rivalries between the English and Welsh kingdoms during this period. His daughter's marriages to Gruffydd ap Llywelyn and King Harold highlight the strategic importance of alliances and the transfer of power through marriage.
