What is the song 'Ludlow Massacre' and its historical context, including the artist who performed it and the event it refers to
The song "Ludlow Massacre" refers to a historical event in which a group of striking coal miners and their families were killed by the Colorado National Guard in Ludlow, Colorado, in 1914. The event was a pivotal moment in the Colorado Coal Strike of 1913-1914, a labor dispute between coal miners and the Colorado Fuel and Iron Company.

The song "Ludlow Massacre" was written and performed by Woody Guthrie, an American folk singer-songwriter. The song is a protest against the violence and exploitation of the coal miners and their families during the strike.

The event itself occurred on April 20, 1914, when the Colorado National Guard attacked a tent colony of striking miners and their families in Ludlow, Colorado, killing an estimated 26 people, including women and children. The event was a turning point in the labor movement in the United States, leading to increased scrutiny of labor practices and the eventual passage of the Fair Labor Standards Act in 1938.
